  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some areas of frost again overnight. Otherwise,\ngenerally tranquil weather is expected to prevail through the \nmiddle portion of next week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our \narea through the middle of next week with continuing mild and \ndry conditions. \n\nOvernight, low temperatures will be similar to this morning's \nlows, except a few degrees higher along and north of I-78. The \ncurrent forecast is for low to mid 30s across interior PA and NJ\nareas, and upper 30s to mid 40s for the urban corridor, \nDelmarva, and coastal areas. So we anticipate some areas of \nfrost developing again tonight, mainly for our PA and NJ areas \noutside of the urban corridor and immediate coast. Another Frost\nAdvisory has been issued for the same areas as last night.\n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist into next \nweek despite surface high pressure. Temperatures are expected to\nincrease back to above normal values early next week, with \nhighs in the mid 60s to low 70s and milder overnights (no \nfrost/freeze potential). Dry weather is expected to persist \nthrough the day Wednesday, when highs will reach well above \nnormal into the low to mid 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances are expected to return late this \nweek into this weekend. \n\nThe ridging that will be in place across the area for the \nmajority of the upcoming week will finally begin to break down \nby Wednesday night into Thursday as a potent upper trough over \ncentral portions of the US closes off and lifts northeastward \ninto portions of the Great Lakes, southern portions of Ontario \nand Quebec, and perhaps northern New England late this week. As \nthis occurs, broad troughing is expected to take shape across \nthe eastern tier of the US, and a few lobes of upper level \nenergy will likely pivot through the region Thursday and into \nthe weekend. At the surface, low pressure centered over the \nMidwest is expected to track northeastward as it weakens into \nThursday. After this time, there is considerable uncertainty. \n\nAt least broad low pressure looks to approach the area Thursday\ninto Friday coincident with the period of greatest height falls\nacross the region. This is expected to bring rain chances back \nto the region, potentially as early as Wednesday night, but more\nlikely Thursday into Friday. For right now, PoPs are in the \n50-60% range for most of the area during this time period. With \na lack of stronger forcing and little to no instability, this \nlooks most likely to be just a beneficial wetting rain to the \nregion.\n\nHeading into this weekend, ensemble guidance indicates negative\nH5 anomalies and generally lowered surface pressure across the \nregion. This indicates that the pattern may remain a bit \nunsettled through Saturday before drier and cooler air begins to\nwork into the region by Sunday.",
